# Define an AI Agent

## Overview

An AI Agent is a declaration: one root reasoning Flow, the request defaults
for that Flow, and the tools the model may call. This Livebook builds a
support Agent, covers each part of the `ai` block and the rules the compiler
enforces, and reads what `explain/0` and `preflight/1` return. By the end,
you can check a request against the declaration without calling a model:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
MyApp.SupportAgent.explain().tools
# => [MyApp.LookupOrder, MyApp.IssueRefund]

{:ok, checked} = MyApp.SupportAgent.preflight(%{input: "Where is order A-1001?"})
checked.params.limits.model_calls
# => 4
```

Run the cells from top to bottom. The model runs offline, so you need no API
key. [Getting Started](getting-started.livemd) is the shorter tour; this page
goes field by field.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.0"}])
```

`plug` provides `Req.Test`, which the offline model uses.

## Define The Tools

Tools are Jido Actions. The model sees each Action's `name`, `description`,
and parameter schema:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.LookupOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/actions/lookup_order",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "lookup_order",
    description: "Look up the shipping status of one order.",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string()})

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{order_id: order_id}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{order_id: order_id, status: "shipped", eta: "2026-11-02"}}
  end
end

defmodule MyApp.IssueRefund do
  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/actions/issue_refund",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "issue_refund",
    description: "Refund part of one order, in cents.",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string(), amount_cents: Zoi.integer(gte: 1)})

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{order_id: order_id, amount_cents: amount_cents}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{order_id: order_id, refunded_cents: amount_cents}}
  end
end
```

## Declare The Agent

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.SupportAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent,
    name: "support_agent",
    id: "my_app/support_agent",
    vsn: 1

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct do
      model "openai:gpt-4o-mini"
      fallback_models ["anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5"]
      instructions "You answer customer-support questions. Look up orders before you answer."

      limits do
        model_calls 4
        tool_rounds 2
      end

      context do
        max_messages 32
      end
    end

    tools do
      action MyApp.LookupOrder
      action MyApp.IssueRefund
    end
  end
end
```

| Declaration | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `use Jido.AI.Agent` | Defines a Jido Agent with a `session` state field and one `jido.ai.request` route. The `id` sets the Signal source to `/my_app/support_agent`. |
| `ai` | Holds one `reasoning` declaration and at most one `tools` block. Nothing else. |
| `reasoning Flow do ... end` | Selects the root Flow and sets its request defaults. |
| `tools` | The ordered tool allowlist. A request can narrow it but can't add to it. |

`use Jido.AI.Agent` also generates these functions:

| Function | Returns |
| --- | --- |
| `reasoning/0` | The root Flow module. |
| `explain/0` | The Flow, its schemas, the defaults, and the tools. |
| `preflight/1` | The validated request params and compiled workflow, without running them. |
| `request_signal!/2`, `request_signal/2` | A `jido.ai.request` Signal with your data. |

## Reasoning Defaults

The `reasoning` block is not a fixed DSL. Its fields are the fields of the
selected Flow's input schema, and nothing else. For
`Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct` those are:

| Field | Default |
| --- | --- |
| `instructions` | `"Answer concisely. Use the available tools when they help. ..."` |
| `model` | `"openai:gpt-4o-mini"` |
| `fallback_models` | `[]` |
| `options` | `%{max_tokens: 128, receive_timeout: 30_000, temperature: 0.0}` |
| `prompt_cache` | `enabled false`, optional `ttl` |
| `stream` | `false` |
| `output_schema` | `nil` |
| `review_tools` | `false` |
| `tool_policy` | `visible`, `hidden`, `review` (all `[]`), `requirement :optional`, `reason nil` |
| `context` | `max_messages 64` (1 to 512), `max_bytes 200_000` |
| `limits` | `model_calls 8`, `tool_rounds 4`, `tool_calls 8`, `retries 1`, `elapsed_ms 120_000`, `tool_output_bytes 16_384`, token and cost limits `nil` |

`input` is also in the schema, but it is the per-request message, so it can't
be a default. Other Flows define other fields; read them from
`explain().input_schema`, or see
[Choose a Reasoning Flow](choose-a-reasoning-flow.md).

Nested objects such as `limits` and `context` use nested blocks. Jido
validates the defaults against the schema at compile time and fills the
fields you left out:

```elixir
MyApp.SupportAgent.explain().defaults.limits
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  model_calls: 4,
  tool_rounds: 2,
  tool_calls: 8,
  retries: 1,
  elapsed_ms: 120_000,
  input_tokens: nil,
  output_tokens: nil,
  total_tokens: nil,
  total_cost: nil,
  tool_output_bytes: 16_384
}
```

Every value must be a compile-time literal. `model System.get_env("MODEL")`
fails with `model requires a compile-time literal value`. Pass runtime
values in the request instead.

## Request Overrides

Each request's Signal data is the Flow input. A field in the request replaces
the Agent default for that field:

```elixir
{:ok, checked} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.preflight(%{
    input: "Where is order A-1001?",
    model: "anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5"
  })

{checked.params.model, checked.params.limits.model_calls}
```

The result is `{"anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5", 4}`.

The replacement is per top-level field. A request `limits` map replaces the
whole `limits` default, and the schema fills the rest:

```elixir
{:ok, checked} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.preflight(%{input: "Where is order A-1001?", limits: %{retries: 0}})

{checked.params.limits.retries, checked.params.limits.model_calls}
```

The result is `{0, 8}`. The Agent's `model_calls 4` is gone. Send the complete
object when you override a nested field.

## Tools

The `tools` block accepts `action Module` and `flow Module` lines. Order is
kept, and the model sees the tools in that order:

```elixir
MyApp.SupportAgent.explain().tools
```

The result is `[MyApp.LookupOrder, MyApp.IssueRefund]`.

The block is an allowlist. A request can narrow it with `tool_policy`
(`visible`, `hidden`, `review`, `requirement`), but it can't add a tool. A
request field named `tools` fails validation with `unrecognized key: tools`.
[Action Tools](action-tools.livemd) covers tool selection, and
[Dynamic Tool Sources](dynamic-tool-sources.md) covers tools that a trusted
host adds at run time.

## Compile-Time Rules

The compiler rejects a malformed `ai` block before the module exists:

| Mistake | Error |
| --- | --- |
| No `ai` block | `AI Agent requires exactly one reasoning Flow` (a `Spark.Error.DslError`) |
| Zero or two `reasoning` declarations | `ai requires exactly one reasoning declaration` |
| Two `tools` blocks | `An Agent can define at most one tools block` |
| Two `ai` blocks | `An Agent can define only one ai block` |
| Anything but `reasoning` or `tools` in `ai` | `ai accepts only reasoning and tools declarations` |
| Anything but `action` or `flow` in `tools` | `tools accepts only action or flow declarations` |
| A field the Flow doesn't define | `reasoning configuration is invalid for Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct at temperature: Flow default field is unknown` |
| A value the schema rejects | `... at limits.model_calls: Flow default does not match its input schema` |
| `input` as a default | `reasoning input cannot be a default` |
| A module that isn't a Flow | `reasoning must reference a compiled Jido Flow module` |

Try one:

```elixir
source = """
defmodule MyApp.TwoToolBlocks do
  use Jido.AI.Agent, name: "two_tool_blocks", id: "my_app/two_tool_blocks", vsn: 1

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct

    tools do
      action MyApp.LookupOrder
    end

    tools do
      action MyApp.IssueRefund
    end
  end
end
"""

try do
  Code.compile_string(source)
rescue
  error in CompileError -> Exception.message(error)
end
```

The result is `"nofile:4: An Agent can define at most one tools block"`. The line is the `ai` block.

`use Jido.AI.Agent` declares the Agent's `state` and `routes` sections
itself. Adding your own `state do` or `routes do` block fails with
`Agent section state can be declared only once`. Add state through a Plugin;
[Agent Authoring](agent-authoring.md) shows how.

## Explain

`explain/0` describes the declaration:

```elixir
explained = MyApp.SupportAgent.explain()

Map.keys(explained) |> Enum.sort()
```

The result is
`[:agent, :defaults, :flow, :input_schema, :output_schema, :reasoning, :tools]`.

| Key | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `agent` | `MyApp.SupportAgent` |
| `reasoning` | `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct` |
| `defaults` | The validated `reasoning` block values |
| `input_schema` | The Flow's Zoi input schema |
| `output_schema` | The Flow's Zoi output schema, `%{state: map, response: map}` |
| `tools` | The `tools` block, in order |
| `flow` | The compiled `%Jido.Flow{}` graph |

`explain/0` reads compiled data. It runs nothing and needs no instance.

## Preflight

`preflight/1` takes request data as a map or keyword list. It merges the
defaults, validates the result against the Flow input schema, checks the
tools, and compiles the Flow into a workflow. It calls no model and runs no
tool:

```elixir
{:ok, checked} = MyApp.SupportAgent.preflight(input: "Where is order A-1001?")

{Map.keys(checked) |> Enum.sort(), checked.workflow.__struct__}
```

The result is
`{[:agent, :flow, :params, :reasoning, :tools, :workflow], Runic.Workflow}`.
`params` is the exact input the Flow would receive: every schema field, with
your defaults and the request merged in.

A bad request returns the schema error:

```elixir
{:error, error} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.preflight(%{input: "Where is order A-1001?", limits: %{model_calls: 99}})

{error.__struct__, Exception.message(error)}
```

The result is
`{Jido.Action.Error.InvalidInputError, "too big: must be at most 16, at limits.model_calls"}`.
A missing `input` gives `"is required, at input"`. Call `preflight/1` in a
controller or test before you queue real model work.

## Offline Model

This cell is the fake provider. It answers ReqLLM's HTTP requests with
OpenAI Responses API JSON, in the order you queue them:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.OfflineModel do
  @moduledoc "Queues fake OpenAI Responses API replies for ReqLLM."

  @doc "Request options that send model calls to this module."
  def options do
    %{
      api_key: "offline-test-key",
      max_retries: 0,
      max_tokens: 128,
      temperature: 0.0,
      req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, MyApp.LLM}]
    }
  end

  @doc "Queues one reply per model call, in order."
  def respond_with(bodies) do
    Enum.each(bodies, fn body ->
      Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, &Req.Test.json(&1, body))
    end)
  end

  @doc "A final text answer."
  def text(text) do
    response([
      %{
        "type" => "message",
        "id" => "msg_1",
        "role" => "assistant",
        "status" => "completed",
        "content" => [%{"type" => "output_text", "text" => text, "annotations" => []}]
      }
    ])
  end

  @doc "A request to call one tool."
  def tool_call(name, arguments, call_id \\ "call_1") do
    response([
      %{
        "type" => "function_call",
        "id" => "fc_" <> call_id,
        "call_id" => call_id,
        "name" => name,
        "arguments" => Jason.encode!(arguments),
        "status" => "completed"
      }
    ])
  end

  defp response(output) do
    %{
      "id" => "resp_1",
      "object" => "response",
      "created_at" => 1,
      "model" => "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18",
      "status" => "completed",
      "error" => nil,
      "incomplete_details" => nil,
      "parallel_tool_calls" => true,
      "output" => output,
      "usage" => %{
        "input_tokens" => 20,
        "input_tokens_details" => %{"cached_tokens" => 0},
        "output_tokens" => 5,
        "output_tokens_details" => %{"reasoning_tokens" => 0},
        "total_tokens" => 25
      }
    }
  end
end
```

`MyApp.OfflineModel.options/0` replaces the whole `options` default for each
request. With a real provider, leave `options` out and set the key ReqLLM
reads, such as `OPENAI_API_KEY`.

## Run A Request

The model asks for `lookup_order`, then answers:

```elixir
MyApp.OfflineModel.respond_with([
  MyApp.OfflineModel.tool_call("lookup_order", %{order_id: "A-1001"}),
  MyApp.OfflineModel.text("Order A-1001 has shipped. It should arrive on 2026-11-02.")
])

agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-1")

signal =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Where is order A-1001?",
    options: MyApp.OfflineModel.options()
  })

{:ok, next_agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

{reply.type, reply.data.status, reply.data.output}
```

The result is
`{"jido.ai.response", :completed, "Order A-1001 has shipped. It should arrive on 2026-11-02."}`.

The Agent's limits applied to this request:

```elixir
Map.take(reply.data, [:model_calls, :tool_rounds, :tool_calls])
```

The result is `%{model_calls: 2, tool_rounds: 1, tool_calls: 1}`, inside the
`model_calls 4` and `tool_rounds 2` defaults. `next_agent.state.session` holds
the conversation; [Sessions and Threads](sessions-and-threads.md) covers it.

## Summary

- An AI Agent has one `ai` block with exactly one `reasoning` declaration and
  at most one `tools` block.
- The `reasoning` block's fields come from the Flow's input schema. Values are
  compile-time literals, checked and completed at compile time.
- A request field replaces the default for that whole top-level field.
- The `tools` block is an ordered allowlist of Actions. Requests can narrow
  it, never extend it.
- `explain/0` returns the Flow, schemas, defaults, and tools.
  `preflight/1` returns validated `params` and a compiled workflow, or the
  schema error, without calling a model.

